Subject: [PATCH 0002] workbench: register the Adze contribution
Refs: ADR-0010
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix.

The change it describes is a single appended import line, and it is still not
written here, for a reason worth being precise about: the surrounding context
lines are what a patch matches on, and they are unknown. A one-line patch with
invented context is a patch that fails to apply while looking trivially correct
in review — the worst combination available.

INTENT
======

Append exactly one import to the workbench's contribution list so that the code
in src/vs/workbench/contrib/adze/ is loaded.

  import './contrib/adze/browser/adze.contribution.js';

That is the entire patch. One line, appended at the end of the existing import
block.

FILES
=====

  src/vs/workbench/workbench.common.main.ts

WHY THIS IS THE WHOLE INTEGRATION
=================================

ADR-0010's central structural claim: our features live in a directory upstream
has no opinion on, so they cannot conflict. src/vs/workbench/contrib/adze/ is not
a file upstream ever edits, never appears in an upstream diff, and therefore never
conflicts — no matter how much code we put there.

The cost of that arrangement is exactly this one line. It is the only place the
fork touches a file upstream actively develops.

RISK, AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTABLE
==============================

workbench.common.main.ts took 31 commits in the 5.8 months measured for ADR-0010.
It will conflict often.

It is nonetheless cheap, because the conflict is always the same shape: upstream
appends imports to the same block we append to. `git rerere` records the
resolution the first time a human does it and replays it afterwards. That is why
.github/workflows/upstream-merge.yml caches .git/rr-cache across runs — without
the cache, a human answers the identical question about the identical line ~50
times a year, which is the toil the merge bot exists to remove.

Two rules keep it cheap:

  * Exactly one line. Two lines in different parts of the file is two conflicts
    and two rerere entries. Everything else goes in adze.contribution.ts, on our
    side of the boundary.
  * Append, never insert. An import placed in the middle of the block conflicts
    with any upstream addition nearby, instead of only with additions at the end.

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

  1. Fetch the checkout: bash apps/ide/scripts/10-fetch-upstream.sh
  2. Create src/vs/workbench/contrib/adze/browser/adze.contribution.ts —
     see apps/ide/contrib/README.md for what belongs in it.
  3. Append the import to the end of the import block in
     src/vs/workbench/workbench.common.main.ts.
  4. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine \
       -- src/vs/workbench/workbench.common.main.ts \
       > apps/ide/patches/0002-workbench-register-adze-contrib.patch

Generate this patch with `-U1`. Fewer context lines means fewer chances for an
upstream edit nearby to invalidate the match, and for an appended line at the end
of a block one line of context is sufficient to place it.
